#!/usr/bin/env node
import { patchFile } from "./_patch-siman-utils.mjs";

let n = 0;
const p = (rel, slug, T) => { n += patchFile(rel, slug, T); };

p("siman_039/mechaber/part-001.txt", "mechaber", {
  "1#main": `One who betroths a woman on condition she has no vows or blemishes, and who must bring proof if they are found. It contains 7 seifim.

One who betroths a woman on condition she has no vows, and one of these three vows is found on her — which people are accustomed to be particular about: that she not eat meat, or not drink wine, or not adorn herself with colored cosmetics — she is not betrothed (and does not need a get) (Tur). The same applies to vows between them and her, which are included in vows people are particular about; if a vow besides these is found on her, even if he says "I am particular even about this" — she is betrothed. If he said to her on condition you have no vow at all, even if it is found she vowed not to eat carobs — she is not betrothed. Some say we are concerned lest she go to a sage and he permit it for her (and therefore she is forbidden to marry without a get).`,
  "2#main": `If she had vows and went to a sage and he permitted them — she is betrothed; even if she is an important woman. When do these words apply? When the sage permitted before the husband knew. But if they became known to the husband before he permitted — the kiddushin are void. Some say if she is an important woman, even if the sage permitted before the husband knew — she is doubtfully betrothed. (And if he betrothed on condition she has no vows and the husband is unspecified — see siman 38 seif 35.)`,
  "3#main": `One who betroths a woman on condition there are no blemishes in her, and one of the disqualifying blemishes in women is found in her — she is not betrothed. If a blemish other than those is found in her, even if he says "I am particular even about this" — she is betrothed.`,
  "4#main": `What are the disqualifying blemishes in women? All blemishes disqualifying kohanim disqualify women, and in addition for women: bad odor, sweat, and mouth odor (and some say also nose odor) (Mordechai in name of RaMaH); thick voice; breasts coarse compared to her peers by a handbreadth; and a handbreadth between breast and breast; dog bite where the place became a scar (meaning the wound healed and skin formed over it and only the wound's mark remained); and a mole (meaning a wart in Old French — Rashi) on the forehead, even if very small and even if there is no hair — this is the mole that permits a woman to kohanim, which stands under the cap on her head: sometimes visible, sometimes not. If always visible — "he saw and was appeased." If always not visible — it is not a blemish.

{Rama: If there is a bathhouse in the city, even hidden blemishes do not void. When do these words apply? When daughters' way is to go to the bathhouse openly; but where daughters go to bathe only at night and hidden — even exposed blemishes void. Epilepsy: if for a fixed time it is a hidden blemish; if not for a fixed time it is an exposed blemish (and see below siman 116 more laws of blemishes). Some say epilepsy and mouth odor — no distinction whether there is a bathhouse in the city or not (R' Yehudah Netiv 23).}`,
  "5#main": `One who betroths a woman without specification, and one of the disqualifying blemishes in women is found on her, or one of vows people are particular about is found on her — she is doubtfully betrothed.`,
  "6#main": `He betrothed her on condition there are no blemishes in her, blemishes were in her and she went to a doctor and he healed her — she is not betrothed. But if the woman stipulated (on condition) there are no blemishes in him, and blemishes were in him and he went to a doctor and healed him — she is betrothed, for there is no disgrace to a man in blemishes already healed, and a woman is not particular about this.`,
  "7#main": `He betrothed her on condition there will not be blemishes in her, blemishes were found in her and the doctor healed them — she is betrothed retroactively.`,
});

p("siman_039/beer-hagolah/part-001.txt", "beer-hagolah", {
  "1#א": `Mishnah Ketubot 72b-73a, as R' Yochanan explains there.`,
  "1#ב": `As Rav Ashi explains there.`,
  "1#ג": `Ran there: because most people are particular about them; see Yoreh De'ah siman 234 which they are.`,
  "1#ד": `Yerushalmi — Rif and Rosh wrote it there; and so Tur and Rambam chapter 7.`,
  "1#ה": `Ran there from Yerushalmi there.`,
  "2#א": `Baraita there 74b, per R' Meir who said a person wants his wife shamed in court.`,
  "2#ב": `Per Rava there who does not distinguish in this — per Rambam there chapter 7 who did not distinguish.`,
  "2#ג": `Tur from words of his father Rosh there.`,
  "2#ד": `Ran in name of Ramban: it is doubtful to him whether halachah is like Rava who distinguishes between important woman, etc. there 75b.`,
  "3#_": `Wording of Rambam there from mishnah and Gemara I noted in baraita seif 1.`,
  "4#א": `There in mishnah explained in Bechorot chapters 7 and 8 and Rambam chapters 6 and 7 Laws of Bi'at Mikdash.`,
  "4#ב": `Baraita Ketubot 75a.`,
  "4#ג": `Statements of amoraim and baraitot there.`,
  "4#ד": `There in baraita as explained there in Gemara.`,
  "5#_": `As Rava's resolution: the tanna is doubtful — regarding money leniently, regarding prohibition stringently there 75b.`,
  "6#א": `Like the first baraita that comes per R' Eliezer and Rava's resolution there 74a.`,
  "6#ב": `Baraita there 75a as explained there in Gemara: a woman in any case is agreeable.`,
  "7#_": `Tur: although in Beit Yosef he wondered on the author about Tur who ruled this law and concluded not like all those rabbanan — nevertheless in his book Bedek HaBayit he retracted and wrote: but I say we should not parse the language precisely to say he is not particular about her but that there will not be afterward; in my humble opinion proof can be brought from mishnah Nedarim 60a "until he goes out," etc.; and the Rav Beit Chadash wrote another matter see there.`,
});

p("siman_039/beit-shmuel/part-001.txt", "beit-shmuel", {
  "1#א": `One of three vows. Even though he can annul her vow under him — all that is a bit of affliction of soul — for it is not proper she vow under him; but in her vow before entering his domain he is particular only in things she fasts much and is degraded by them — Ran.`,
  "1#ב": `She is not betrothed. If he wishes to forgive — explained in previous siman kiddushin are void retroactively and other kiddushin are not needed. What is written siman 60 that he must betroth anew — discusses mistaken kiddushin of another type; or discusses particular when he heard — forgiveness does not help, as written there in name of Ran.`,
  "1#ג": `And the same for vows between them. Chelkat Mechokek wrote: not all vows between them explained in Yoreh De'ah siman 234, but great matters such as "I am removed from Jews" and similar that most people are particular about — and so Ran.`,
  "1#ד": `And some say, etc. In Yerushalmi there is a dispute; Rashba and Ran ruled she may marry and we are not concerned lest she go to permit, for she does not ruin herself to make mamzerim; Ri'az ruled stringently we are concerned lest she go to permit. Even though below siman 143 in condition "return and do not do" we do not say she will transgress the condition — for certainly she will not transgress to ruin herself; only here we are concerned lest she not want the vow on her all her days and go to permit — ruin comes even if his intent is only for mitzvah, not like Chelkat Mechokek who entered forcedly. However difficult: this concern before husband knew — afterward sage's permission does not help as written nearby; if so why need reason she will go to sage? Say lest husband forgive and not be particular — then kiddushin void retroactively as previous siman he can forgive even on condition without money. Perhaps from this Ran inferred Yerushalmi disputes Tosafot and Yerushalmi holds even after husband knew sage's permission helps; thus Yerushalmi discusses even when husband knew — nevertheless need reason lest she go to permit. However in Ri'az it implies he holds Yerushalmi does not dispute Tosafot; Yerushalmi discusses when husband did not know; nevertheless we do not say lest he not be particular — presumption is to be particular; and he explicitly stipulated — also not concerned lest he forgive intending to ruin them.`,
  "2#א": `But if they became known to husband. So Tosafot, Tur, Ri'az; Yerushalmi does not dispute this as written nearby; and so Rosh's view — therefore Beit Yosef brought Yerushalmi and Tosafot adjacent, not like Beit Yosef. But Ran is stringent and holds permission helps even after husband knew; even husband's forgiveness does not help after he was particular in first chapter; nevertheless sage's permission is different — uproots vow from root as though she never vowed.`,
  "2#ב": `Important woman. Meaning when she says "I do not want a woman who vows" — for Rava's answer it is agreeable to him that kiddushin take effect at all with important woman, for where he need not divorce and his relatives not forbidden — this is meaning of important woman. For Rava's answer there even important woman is betrothed; Geonim doubt whether halachah like Rava against Rabbah who is later, or like Rabbah who is his teacher — therefore with important woman she is doubtfully betrothed. However it appears from Rosh, Tur, Ri'az they hold main view is like Rava, only stringently ruled like Rabbah; if so if he betrothed afterward she is betrothed to second biblically — as proven first chapter Bava Metzia where Rosh ruled like Abaye against Rabbah regarding signed witnesses benefit him; if so what is written here doubtfully betrothed — this is Ran as Ran wrote in name of Ramban; for Rosh it is not doubtful kiddushin. See Shitah Mekubetzet 174d: even non-important woman is doubtful kiddushin for halachah is doubtful; one cannot understand the doubt in non-important woman — perhaps it discusses permission after husband knew and doubtful in Ran's reasoning.`,
  "2#ג": `And husband unspecified. I already wrote previous siman these laws; there are two reasons: first reason — he betroths at time of bringing in — therefore requires witnesses of seclusion; second reason — he forgives the condition and first kiddushin take effect and other kiddushin not needed and witnesses of seclusion not needed. Rambam holds second reason; Tur holds first reason and requires witnesses of seclusion. Below explained: if he did not stipulate and betrothed and vows or blemishes found; likewise if betrothed and married without specification — she needs get from doubt and has no ketubah; if he stayed with her she has ketubah. If he said to her: since first ketubah is void how can she collect this ketubah? Say his intent: if he will not be particular afterward her ketubah stands as previous siman. Likewise if stayed with her afterward — certainly betrothed and certainly needs get — from reason when he stipulated nothing it is doubt whether he is particular; if stayed afterward it is clarified he is not particular — certainly betrothed.`,
  "3#_": `Found in her another blemish. Possible if he said explicitly on condition there is no blemish at all — then even other blemishes impede like vows if he said on condition you have no vow at all.`,
  "4#א": `Disqualifying in kohanim. See Rambam Laws of Bi'at Mikdash chapter 8; regarding one who urinates in bed or leper — Beit Yosef ruled it is a blemish; in Bedek HaBayit he left difficult; Taz wrote must give ketubah but can divorce against her will even nowadays there is cherem of Rabbenu Gershom; man urinating in bed wrote in Be'er Sheva it is not a blemish.`,
  "4#ב": `Bad odor. So Rambam wrote; in Tur not so — it appears difficult for Rav Ashi this is zav contamination as explained; if so disqualifies kohen and automatically included in disqualifying blemishes; large mole like issar or if has hair — per Rambam specifically standing on forehead; per Tur even on rest of body.`,
  "4#ג": `He saw and was appeased. Even though he stipulated on condition no blemish — nevertheless we say he did not stipulate on this — Tosafot. However per Mordechai and Hagahot Alfasi even exposed blemish — as long as she did not enter husband's domain we say he did not see; bathhouse inspection does not apply before erusin; entire mishnah discusses nissuin. Rambam here regarding kiddushin does not distinguish exposed from other blemishes; only in chapter 8 regarding ketubah he distinguishes; Maggid wrote in Bedek HaBayit it seems to him even exposed blemishes void kiddushin — therefore I would be stringent even exposed. Tur in this siman wrote laws explained in Gemara if blemishes found after erusin and it is unknown when born — as below siman 115; but author wrote in Bedek HaBayit these laws do not apply to this siman — here regarding kiddushin if blemishes found after erusin and she claims born after erusin — we do not say here "found here was" to void kiddushin; only needs get from him; only regarding ketubah we distinguish. Therefore he omitted these laws here in Shulchan Arukh. Beit Chadash wrote even Tur who wrote these laws in this siman — his intent only regarding ketubah not voiding kiddushin. In my humble opinion it is proven per Tur blemishes void kiddushin — for he wrote she must return kiddushin per sugya there as siman 50; and there explained where she needs get they do not return kiddushin lest one say no get needed — we learn per Tur no get needed; why wrote she returns kiddushin? It appears proof for Tur's words: we extract money per this reasoning here "found here was" as chapter HaMedir and Choshen Mishpat siman 232 — if so likewise no get needed from him; and there discusses where no definite claim; here she claims definitely blemishes born afterward — therefore regarding get we are stringent and she needs get; for per Rashi and Tosafot sugya there discusses even if father claims definitely we say "found here was" and extract kiddushin from him. However if blemishes found after nissuin — one can say she needs get even if husband brings proof blemishes were before erusin; nevertheless needs get from him as previous siman lest at time of bringing in he forgave condition. But blemishes found after erusin — one can say no get needed for these reasons. However siman 117 explains per Ramban and Rashba: father claiming definitely need not bring proof — we learn here we do not say against definite claims; and sugya there per their explanation discusses where no definite claims. If so when she claims definitely blemishes born after erusin — even regarding ketubah her definite claim does not help since money belongs to her father; nevertheless regarding get her definite claim helps and she needs get. Found in dispute of these poskim — Tosafot, Ramban, Rashba below siman 77 this law depends; Rosh and Tur hold like Tosafot — therefore per Tur no get needed; therefore Tur wrote these laws in this siman. Per Ramban and Rashba when she claims definitely she needs get. But one cannot bring proof she needs get because she says blemishes born after erusin — if so it is as saying "you betrothed me" and needs get — one can say here it is worse because we say "found here was" and proven she lies; after matchmaking he did not betroth her — we do not say his field was washed away — so Rosh responsum and siman 50. Found per this: if he did not stipulate and blemish found after matchmaking — he can cancel matchmaking and exempt without fine; for if found after nissuin need not give ketubah — if so after matchmaking exempt without fine; see siman 117.`,
  "4#ד": `And if always not visible. Specifically small mole without hair — not blemish when not visible; but large mole like issar or if has hair — even if not visible it is blemish; and scar even if not visible. But Rambam wrote mole is not blemish except on facial skin — Chelkat Mechokek.`,
  "4#ה": `That daughters do not go to bathhouse. Meaning and all the more without bathhouse they do not go outside at all; discusses he betrothed via agent and did not see her at kiddushin — therefore even exposed blemish voids kiddushin — Chelkat Mechokek.`,
  "4#ו": `Epilepsy and mouth odor. Likewise sweat and thick voice — she can be careful a short time while in bathhouse.`,
  "5#א": `Woman without specification. If she is ailonit — certainly no get needed as Tosafot wrote there; see siman 173.`,
  "5#ב": `Doubtfully betrothed. Chelkat Mechokek wrote: discusses immediately when he sees blemish he is particular and does not want her; but if silent when blemish known to him — even if screamed afterward — kiddushin are certain; likewise if betrothed without specification and married and had relations without specification — betrothed and no ketubah. In Gemara per Rabbenu Chananel she needs get from their words; per Rava it is doubtful kiddushin; Rambam and Tur ruled like Rava; not like Beit Chadash who wrote Tur's words doubtful kiddushin because he doubted whether halachah like Rabbenu Chananel or Rava — rejected.`,
  "6#_": `And healed her. Even if healed before husband knew — nevertheless not betrothed; even in vows permission before husband knew helps because blemishes are repugnant in his eyes even after healed. If blemishes were before kiddushin and healed — betrothed for he did not stipulate on this — Chelkat Mechokek. If leper before kiddushin or epileptic from childhood — possibly blemish, concern lest return leper and epileptic under him — Chelkat Mechokek siman 116. Tosafot wrote: do not say healing does not help because condition language was on condition no blemishes and there were — if so when he stipulated on condition none in him why healing helps? Rather reason is repugnance; in man when healed not repugnant in her eyes. We hear from this: even condition she stipulated with him — if condition not fulfilled kiddushin void; only when healed we say condition fulfilled. If he stipulated on condition there will not be blemishes — even if healed it is as condition not fulfilled. If blemishes in him, known to her, afterward healed — doubtful in Chelkat Mechokek whether kiddushin void. It appears proof kiddushin void: sugya challenged and taught "go to sage to permit, go to doctor and heal — not betrothed"; and did not resolve it discusses already known to him — therefore must establish after known; if so difficult from end: taught "he went and vow permitted or healed — betrothed"; and after known to her does not help if healed. However one can say that which halachah not established discusses after known — regarding doctor and heal not betrothed even if not known — we learn regarding sage too discusses not known. If she stipulated on condition no vow in him and vows found — not betrothed even if sage permitted after known to her; for vows not from repugnance but reason: when he betrothed and stipulated on condition no vows and vows found and known — sage's permission does not help because he thinks perhaps no permission for vow as Tosafot and Rosh; if so even if she stipulated law is same — for I already wrote whenever condition not fulfilled even if she stipulated kiddushin void.`,
  "7#א": `On condition there will not be blemishes, etc. Implies he is not particular about this blemish now but that he not leave a blemish in her afterward; even though Tosafot, Rosh, and other poskim wrote particularity is not in language — Tosafot's intent everything depends on fulfillment of condition as written nearby; when he stipulated on condition none in her and she healed — condition not fulfilled; in such case condition fulfilled — not like Beit Yosef who brought Tosafot dispute this law; see Bedek HaBayit what he wrote. All this per our text; but Beit Chadash emends Tur. However even per Beit Chadash's text this law seems true per what is written in such case condition fulfilled; and Chelkat Mechokek wrote it is doubtful kiddushin.`,
  "7#ב": `And doctor healed them. Possible even after known to him it helps; here if healed condition fulfilled that he not leave in her; here not relevant to say repugnant in his eyes for he is not particular except not to leave; however one can say when known he thought no cure and it is like vows when known permission does not help because he thinks no permission as written nearby.`,
});

p("siman_039/baer-hetev/part-001.txt", "baer-hetev", {
  "1#א": `Betrothed. If he wishes to forgive — kiddushin void retroactively and other kiddushin not needed — see Beit Shmuel.`,
  "1#ב": `Between them. Not all vows between them explained in Yoreh De'ah siman 234, but great matters such as "removed from Jews" that most people are particular about — Chelkat Mechokek and Ran.`,
  "3#_": `Other. Possible if he said explicitly on condition no blemish at all — then even other blemishes impede like vows if he said on condition you have no vow — Beit Shmuel.`,
  "4#א": `In kohanim. See Rambam Laws of Bi'at Mikdash chapter 8. Woman who always urinates in bed, or leper — Beit Yosef ruled blemish; in Bedek HaBayit left difficult; Taz wrote must give ketubah but can divorce against her will even nowadays cherem Rabbenu Gershom; see below siman 117 seif 5. Man urinating in bed wrote in Be'er Sheva 172b not blemish — see Hagahot Isserles. If she has pimples on head — if many considered blemishes; if few and small possibly women's way and not considered blemish — Ranach chapter 1 siman 102.`,
  "4#ב": `And was appeased. Should be stringent even exposed blemishes — as long as she did not enter husband's domain we say he did not see — Beit Shmuel. He brought: if he did not stipulate at all and blemish found after matchmaking — can cancel matchmaking exempt without fine; for if found after nissuin need not give ketubah — if so after matchmaking exempt without fine.`,
  "4#ג": `Visible. Specifically small mole without hair — not blemish when not visible; large mole like issar or if has hair — even if not visible it is blemish; scar even if not visible. Rambam wrote mole not blemish except facial skin — Chelkat Mechokek, Beit Shmuel.`,
  "4#ד": `Mouth odor. Likewise sweat and thick voice — she can be careful short time in bathhouse — Chelkat Mechokek, Beit Shmuel.`,
  "5#_": `From doubt. Chelkat Mechokek wrote: discusses immediately when he sees blemish he is particular; but if silent when blemish known — even if screamed afterward — kiddushin certain — see Beit Shmuel and Hagahot Isserles.`,
  "6#א": `Her. Even if healed before husband knew — nevertheless not betrothed. However if at kiddushin blemishes already healed that were before kiddushin — betrothed for he did not stipulate. If leper before kiddushin or epileptic from childhood — possibly blemish, concern lest return — Chelkat Mechokek, Beit Shmuel.`,
  "6#ב": `And healed. If known to her and afterward healed — Chelkat Mechokek concludes whether kiddushin void. If he stipulated on condition no vow in him and vows found — not betrothed even if sage permitted after known — Beit Shmuel see there.`,
  "7#_": `Retroactively. Chelkat Mechokek wrote it is doubtful — see there.`,
});

p("siman_039/beur-hagra/part-001.txt", "beur-hagra", {
  "1#א": `That people are accustomed, etc. For they are vows of affliction of soul; and this is meaning of "and the same for vows," etc. — therefore husband annuls them.`,
  "1#ב": `And she is not, etc. and some say, etc. and therefore, etc. Dispute in Yerushalmi; Rosh brought chapter 7 end siman 13 "there are conditions," etc.`,
  "2#א": `She had vows, etc. We hold like R' Meir for thus Tanna Kamma of mishnah Gittin there holds like him — Rosh.`,
  "2#ב": `And even, etc. All per Rabbah for Rabbah is teacher of Rava; and in Choshen Mishpat siman 391 seif 5.`,
  "2#ג": `When do these words apply, etc. Since mishnah does not distinguish and says "she is not betrothed" forever — Rosh; only in such case.`,
  "2#ד": `And some say, etc. It is doubtful whether halachah like Rava from Abaye and Rava onward halachah like later ones — and in Choshen Mishpat there.`,
  "3#_": `Even though he said, etc. From what is written "all blemishes," etc. only in these; and as written regarding vows as above seif 1.`,
  "4#א": `And some say, etc. From what is written Avodah Zarah 1a "these that were born," etc. and these that are forced, etc.; Gemara applies nose odor — all the more in woman. That which is written here mouth odor — to include even if she holds pepper in mouth as written here in Gemara — Maharam.`,
  "4#ב": `And if there is, etc. Mishnah 75b; Tosafot there s.v. aval, etc.; appears to Rashba, etc.; and so Ri'az in his name.`,
  "4#ג": `When do these words apply, etc. Rosh and Tur; and so Semag; and below siman 117 seif 7.`,
  "4#ד": `Epilepsy, etc. Avodah Zarah 1a.`,
  "4#ה": `Some say, etc. For these are not recognizable in bathhouse.`,
  "5#_": `One who betroths, etc. 73b Rava said tanna, etc.; and although stated to Shmuel likewise to Rav — although it appears per Rav certainly betrothed as written do not say, etc.; nevertheless from what is written 74a "this was an incident" and was not, etc. only doubt; and below siman 38 seif 35 and Ran.`,
  "6#_": `He betrothed her, etc. Baraita there and no dispute at all.`,
  "7#_": `He betrothed her, etc. Tur; and learned thus from Rosh there beginning seif 63: do not explain specifically, etc.; only one who parsed explicitly in language on condition there will not be in her — certainly betrothed.`,
});

p("siman_039/beit-meir/part-001.txt", "beit-meir", {
  "1#_": `And some say — see Chelkat Mechokek; Beit Shmuel wrote even if her intent is not to ruin only for mitzvah — what is this reasoning? There is no mitzvah to permit her vow; she ruins herself and will be in prohibition forever; and main point is like Chelkat Mechokek.`,
  "2#_": `Beit Shmuel wrote — see Shitah Mekubetzet, etc. Per Rashi I wanted to say Shitah Mekubetzet's view: why we are doubtful like Rava — automatically for us who hold "he gave a finger between them" even non-important woman there is doubt; for Rava is challenged: in important woman who says "I do not want a vowed woman" — known this language only in vows of affliction; see Maharshal who explained main reason we are concerned she will vow under him — he proved well and Gemara challenges — not like Perishah. If so what if she vows further — he can annul; must be concern she will vow and from anger he will make her deaf and be forced to divorce and he does not want prohibition of relatives. Difficult: Scripture teaches per this even non-important can claim he does not want to give ketubah — therefore like forced to establish in one tanna neither R' Meir nor R' Eliezer; also forced to establish in one opinion that holds "she gave" — we establish in two reasons: one in important woman, one in non-important. Found per Rava's reasoning for us who hold "he gave a finger" — even non-important are doubtful. However this is not so — if so for all challenged even non-important; even if he gives — nevertheless can claim he does not want betrothal because of feast he toils and loses — nissuin feast. Or Rava's reasoning to void kiddushin is only in important woman because of loss of relatives, not because of monetary loss — therefore simpler as Beit Shmuel explained.`,
  "3#_": `He saw and was appeased — Beit Shmuel wrote; however per Mordechai and Hagahot Alfasi, etc. — therefore I would be stringent even exposed blemishes. Meaning: if she extended hand and accepted kiddushin from another — see Mordechai and Hagahot Rosh — appears explicitly opposite: mole on forehead he holds even R' Meir admits we say he saw and was appeased. Apparently wrote to resolve Tosafot's difficulty s.v. vechachamim perhaps baraita is R' Meir; and Tur's view to Beit Yosef appears text reads "no exposed blemishes" not "there are" — implying specifically exposed blemishes he distinguishes between their way to go by day and not — even exposed void; unlike mole on forehead always betrothed because certainly saw. Since Mordechai appears mole where sometimes visible is included in exposed — challenged: what difference? Or standing on forehead — nevertheless for Rabbanan challenged: this is exposed blemish — therefore Tosafot moved from this; see chapter 8 and strained to establish conclusion. But per R' Meir and R' Yehudah explained specifically after nissuin, etc. Possibly also from reasoning he wrote thus; nevertheless proven baraita tanna added mole when claiming while still betrothed — if after nissuin per Rabbanan he cannot claim because called exposed blemish; presumably baraita for conclusion of most poskim: betrothed woman does not need ketubah for we require voiding kiddushin — as Mordechai wrote she is not betrothed even if bathhouse in city; and since he strained change "standing on forehead" — we learn mole on forehead certainly betrothed because certainly saw and was appeased. Without this proven from "he saw and was appeased" difficulty — certainly betrothed; otherwise only from this would be doubtful — does not immediately challenge; nevertheless needed to teach tanna added small mole standing on forehead so not certainly betrothed. From all this proven Mordechai's view also regarding standing on forehead explicitly certainly betrothed even when claiming while betrothed — no room to be stringent in my opinion. However Rambam's language implies even standing on forehead likewise if not betrothed — for he began "what are," etc. and mole on forehead even close to head hair meaning standing on forehead; and implies all the more far from hair not visible; Ran and Hagahot Maimoniyot left difficult; Hagahot Maimoniyot wondered except he wrote because he relied it will be explained chapter 25 if blemish-er knew he cannot claim blemish claim; when they said "he saw and was appeased" it is regarding ketubah — this appears his view. Beit Yosef wondered on Hagahot Maimoniyot who forced this; say we learn from his custom that if place where women go, etc. — he saw and was appeased; if place where custom to hide — even if mole inside she is not betrothed; but in Bedek HaBayit he wavered on Tur's method and learned from Rambam in kiddushin no distinction; Beit Shmuel took Bedek HaBayit as main and aligned with Hagahot Maimoniyot — learned from both to be stringent even exposed blemish. From his words it appears his view likewise mole on forehead explicitly law is thus; already Hagahot Maimoniyot wondered on Hagahot Maimoniyot's language how it appears contradicting himself — strained unbelievable things. However on understanding Beit Yosef in Rambam and Beit Yosef, Chelkat Mechokek, Beit Shmuel in Hagahot Maimoniyot — in my humble opinion wonder: Bedek HaBayit wrote since Rambam did not distinguish in chapter 7 — appears his view regarding kiddushin even exposed void as he wrote these matters only regarding if she leaves without ketubah not voiding kiddushin; and Rambam appears not to distinguish; if so Rambam's intent: per his view always even exposed void kiddushin and permitted forever — for he wrote betrothed on condition and blemishes found — not betrothed and did not distinguish; how possible regarding money say he saw and was appeased obligated in ketubah yet permitted without get? Rather opposite found regarding get — prohibition stringently, money leniently; only Hagahot Maimoniyot explained mishnah at least discusses betrothed on condition and married without specification we say per Rav condition extends to kiddushin and money stands on condition — one can say on what he saw appeased unlike voiding kiddushin where only betrothed on condition and found — no distinction as Tosafot distinguish; rather like Tosafot's difficulty 75b s.v. aval: since explicitly stipulated even exposed blemish void; if so fine to be stringent like Rambam even standing on forehead since per his view certainly not betrothed since stipulated. Nevertheless difficult: from where Gemara proves even standing under cap is blemish — perhaps baraita only discusses voiding kiddushin where betrothed on condition? Can force: one from tanna added on them implies refers to what mishnah said after "she leaves without ketubah all disqualifying in kohanim" — implies also regarding ketubah law applies to what was added; further includes mole with contamination and others — implies one manner. Nevertheless in my humble opinion see Beit Yosef in Shulchan Arukh: since he omitted and did not distinguish at all between exposed and hidden — holds like Rambam since he stipulated even exposed void; from my difficulties Gemara possibly holds to distinguish: standing on forehead explicitly has precedence over other exposed — certainly saw and was appeased; even though not stipulated on them — stipulation language of Tosafot — and she is certainly betrothed; this is Mordechai's view giving mole on forehead precedence over other exposed. But he disputes: Mordechai gave standing under cap law of exposed and explained after nissuin exposed do not void — implies even where voiding applies after nissuin such as betrothed and married on condition — proven he holds after nissuin since way to examine we say Tosafot's language he did not stipulate on them; Shulchan Arukh from unspecified standing on forehead not betrothed implies no distinction and always even all exposed void wherever applicable even after nissuin — here room to be concerned for Mordechai's view: after nissuin he saw and was appeased even standing on forehead sometimes visible; but to be concerned for Rambam's view standing on forehead explicitly since we see Shulchan Arukh though lenient and did not distinguish exposed implying holds even exposed void since stipulated — nevertheless regarding standing on forehead ruled he saw and was appeased not like Rambam — we too in my opinion need not be concerned except to be stringent she is only doubtfully betrothed — examine. In Tur it says initially where proof on father if he does not bring must return kiddushin money; Rambam and Shulchan Arukh omitted; per Rambam possibly from Beit Shmuel's reason these laws do not apply to this siman, etc. — see there; therefore also she does not return kiddushin money lest they say "but"; Shulchan Arukh who rules siman 50 and in Ketzot — see Chelkat Mechokek; if we say he holds this law only in first wording of Rav Yehudah regarding donkey-driver's proof and tanna taught bride in father's house — then for kiddushin as Gemara language; unlike conclusion when R' Meir came "all born in his domain" — bride in father-in-law's house and kiddushin; nevertheless difficult: in Choshen Mishpat siman 224 explains like Rambam even in conclusion donkey-driver's proof and Ran explained his method "bride in father's house and kiddushin" — perhaps Rambam and Shulchan Arukh per their methods chapter 7 law 8: one who betroths woman without specification and vow or blemish found — doubtfully betrothed; meaning he was concerned for Tosafot's answer Rava who said tanna is doubtful — money leniently per Rashi extracts from fellow on him; prohibition stringently; and holds likewise betrothed alone law is thus; if so difficulty why we conclude all born in his domain, etc. bride in father's house and kiddushin — fine presumption when blemish clarified; unlike doubt if people generally particular about blemishes who is proven — say leniency for defendant — holder now of kiddushin money who is father; fine first wording donkey-driver's proof from presumption of 100 cows as Tosafot explained; likewise bride in father's house and kiddushin — husband has presumption of 100 cows; therefore discusses also claiming perhaps or as Tosafot explained it is money in your hand; as Ran in name of Razah: since doubt born in his money; unlike conclusion main reason because here found — must establish in betrothed on condition; perhaps therefore Tosafot preferred their explanation and conclude s.v. v'tanna tonah meaning bride in father-in-law's house — appeared difficult to establish specifically explicit on condition as their view s.v. aval in blemishes wrote meaning bride in father-in-law's house to avoid establishing and kiddushin; see Maharsha; but for Rambam as above; precise reader of Rambam and Shulchan Arukh will see they established this law on betrother without specification — fine they omitted return money since we rule like Rava that even erusin alone there is doubt; unlike ketubah where we say here found and before erusin were in her — do not establish body on its presumption any doubt lest appeased — cannot extract money; but kiddushin money is as holder in everyone's domain we follow present presumption; if from presumption person appeased in blemishes she is not silenced to extract money unlike Tur who established law in betrothed on condition — fine writes also kiddushin money must return; however Beit Shmuel wrote Tur holds no get needed and concludes but one cannot bring proof, etc. here we say here found, etc. proven she lies — in my humble opinion astounding words: in her speech she is believed about herself more than hundred witnesses to make herself forbidden; and from presumption say she lies; and more than woman in presumption fully single if says you betrothed me needs get; in my opinion even if she retracts after "she leaves without ketubah" and gives excuse perhaps her claim — but to extract ketubah still difficult; see siman 6; his proof from extracting money also astounding — there main reason presumption of 100 cows per that sugya donkey-driver's proof as Tosafot explained; if proof from kiddushin also from that reason and specifically perhaps see there — examine. This too reason Tosafot in my opinion conclude v'tanna tonah bride in father-in-law's house — not reasonable for conclusion to establish mishnah specifically in perhaps and definite claimant claim not reasonable to extract kiddushin money; see Tosafot s.v. al ba'al hachamor and Tur who wrote even father claims definitely — truly difficult; but to say therefore she lies even if not retracting and leaves without get — God forbid in my opinion; even though I again found in Chadashim of Ra'ah exactly his view — nevertheless already rejected from mouths of all early ones see there chapter 8; even in perhaps and perhaps in my opinion needs get from their words and not close doubt to him or her — she in presumption single; nevertheless needs get; in Rashi appears even here found language only to impair body presumption — all the more claimant claims born after betrothal — in my opinion simple to all needs get; if from difficulty on Tur how return kiddushin money see siman 50.`,
  "4#_": `{Hagahah} daughters do not go — Chelkat Mechokek wrote discusses he betrothed via agent; meaning what if she goes hidden — at kiddushin time he saw; if from this in my opinion not — Mordechai already explained blemish similar to mole standing on forehead sometimes visible also included in exposed; all the more hand called exposed — possibly investigate via neighbors if she does not go hidden; nevertheless certainly cannot challenge such as this — he saw at kiddushin time and was appeased as challenged on forehead — impossible not visible at kiddushin. However his law clear even on forehead when betrothed via agent and said on condition no blemish — what if agent saw? Obviously not betrothed; if so difficult what Gemara challenges "he saw" — perhaps difference when betrothed via agent; per Rashi occurred to me to derive from Chelkat Mechokek siman 232: all who agreed in city it is blemish voids sale — this does not discuss agent; as we hold there siman 227 if deceived in anything sale void because can say "for your benefit I sent you," etc. — likewise buyer of thing with any blemish.`,
  "5#_": `And the same as her — and fine challenged "he saw" — if difference of agent why tanna added even any blemish voids kiddushin see there. But I see this is not from what we learned and brought in tractate Kiddushin 41b: diminished ten or added ten — his terumah is terumah; Tosafot wrote: but here since many people customarily tithe generously — can say "therefore I estimated you" and voided your mind to my mind because I estimated you, end quote — behold in what people's custom not to be particular he cannot say "for your benefit," etc.; all the more in blemishes Chazal agreed void his mind he cannot claim "for your benefit," etc.; not similar to ona'ah less than sixth for conversely every person does not waive willingly; all the more other than where deceived they said sages bedi'eved forgive; on agent's act need not forgive — difference light and clear; my difficulty therefore forced as I forced above in Rambam's view; law of matchmaking learn from here; see Beit Shmuel s.k. 11 — examine.`,
});

p("siman_039/chokhmat-shlomo/part-001.txt", "chokhmat-shlomo", {
  "1#_": `Seif 6 — he betrothed on condition no blemishes, etc., and healed her she is not betrothed, etc. NB it appears to me specifically in those blemishes explained above those disqualifying in women — then Tosafot's reason fits well that even after healed repugnant in his eyes. But in other blemishes even if he stipulated explicitly also not betrothed as Gemara implies regarding vows, etc., as Perishah wrote there; nevertheless if she went to doctor and healed — betrothed for since not so repugnant and without specification kiddushin are fine; only if stipulated it is like other conditions; but if healed it is like "he went to sage and healed" she is betrothed [below I expanded in resolving Gemara's words]. So it appears to me — examine. See Beit Shmuel s.k. 17 what he wrote "however one can say," etc.; it appears in my humble opinion this resolution does not suffice at all: one, I already found in Maharam Schiff one should not say this resolution at all — still Rava is challenged who resolves in important woman — if so with doctor and healed — therefore even non-important betrothed — if so tanna not similar — if so Beit Shmuel's difficulties remain; further it appears per what I proved in my Mahadura Kama on Avodah Zarah: in other blemishes besides those in mishnah — he stipulated no blemish at all on him; since particular even about anything — nevertheless healing helps; if so Beit Shmuel's difficulties remain: discusses after husband knew and said no blemish at all on him and other blemishes found — even if healing helps — nevertheless after husband knew does not help like vows and disqualifying in kohanim — he did not want to teach us — already heard from other baraita; rather certainly also when woman stipulated healing does not help after husband knew; in what I wrote resolved for me what was difficult in Rava's words resolving important woman — not similar to doctor, etc.; per above Rava establishes when said any blemish and other blemishes found — nevertheless not betrothed in important woman, etc. But truly Beit Shmuel's words astounding in my opinion — why force into narrow breach? Per what he wrote afterward immediately in vows certainly permission does not help after woman knew she thinks perhaps no permission for vow — if so how establish vows discusses after known? If so end "he went to sage" also discusses after known — why betrothed certainly? Halachah and "he went" discuss one manner for distinction between man and woman; why force to say initially because "went to doctor" not establish thus and leave vows that preceded? Truly appears both in vows and blemishes permission/healing does not help after woman knew — also in blemishes applicable she thinks perhaps no cure for his blemish as Beit Shmuel wrote in his honor at end of siman; not worse than vows — so appears correct for law — examine well.`,
});

p("siman_039/pitchei-teshuva/part-001.txt", "pitchei-teshuva", {
  "1#_": `Another blemish. Avnei HaTzedek in name of Beit Shmuel; see responsum Shevut Yaakov part 3 siman 83 who wrote even though Beit Shmuel wrote thus as possible — in his humble opinion it is simple; and so explained in Tur, etc. see there.`,
  "2#_": `Disqualifying in kohanim. See responsum Noda BiYehudah siman 53: asked regarding matched woman who due to illness lost sense of hearing — can groom cancel match since all disqualifying in kohanim disqualify in women; in kohanim we said chapter 7 Bechorot deaf-mute — doubt if this deaf specifically does not hear and does not speak like ordinary deaf, or even does not hear alone considered blemish. Wrote from mishnah language implies means does not hear alone for we said both in person and animal; though can reject — all this in kohanim; in women no doubt this is great blemish. What they did not count among additional in women because there discusses she had before kiddushin — otherwise his field washed away; if at kiddushin she was deaf how betrothed unless needed hints — then he saw and was appeased; unless say betrothed via her agent or minor via father — not common; but in matchmaking where no field washed away — simple no greater blemish; can cancel match even if not actually deaf only needs shout loudly — considered blemish for when speaks on intimacy matters neighbors will hear. All per circumstance: if can hear raised quiet voice not heard room to outside — not blemish; if must speak very loud voice — certainly blemish in woman; can cancel without fine; even if seized does not help — no doubt see there; see Yad HaMelech chapter 7 Laws of Forbidden Relations law 10 what he wrote; see responsum Chut HaMeshulash siman 220: one wished to cancel match because bride has nose longer than proper — size of his small finger as explained chapter Eleh Mumim. Wrote: appears if elongation not extreme so majority do not mock — his words nothing, etc. Clear from his words: without this matchmaker explicitly stipulating no blemish — even if said explicitly no deficiency — no equal doubt and certainly obligated fine if wishes retract; if stipulated explicitly no blemish — doubtful and do not extract money from doubt; if groom has groom's money can seize fine money (wrote there also regarding kiddushin similarly doubtful if we say with stipulation not betrothed with slightly long nose not mocked) — clear there. Wrote further: likewise reverse incident — groom known bride has blemish — eyes very closed so to see opposite must force open eyes with effort; groom left court scot-free; though not found this blemish in kohanim for not blind nor small eyes like goose — nevertheless among people great blemish and great change, etc.; in omissions wrote likewise if found in woman tataranit (illness cannot smell) — though not blemish in kohanim — in woman blemish for must deal with dishes and taste, etc.; so proven Gemara Bava Batra 146; however per Rashi there no proof see there. Per Beit Shmuel below siman 90 s.k. 22 wrote we do not find tataranit is blemish, etc. see there. (b) handbreadth between breasts, etc. — in margin Shulchan Arukh Gaon R' Akiva Eiger ztz"l NB: all the more if she has no breasts at all — Chelkat Mechokek below siman 117 s.k. 10 end quote.`,
  "3#_": `He saw and was appeased. Per Beit Shmuel s.k. 11 regarding if blemishes found after erusin and she claims born after erusin — wrote proven Tur's view holds here found here was also voids kiddushin and does not require get; wrote at end but one cannot bring proof she needs get because she says blemishes born after erusin — if so as saying you betrothed me and needs get — one can say here worse because we say here found here was proven she lies, end quote. See Sefer Perishah in Kovetz on tractate Ketubot siman 177 who wrote on this his words very astounding and I was astonished at the appearance — why say thus? We find many times even with several presumptions we also say she made herself a piece of prohibition and no need to multiply proofs; what he parsed from Tur's language — conversely can parse opposite since wrote she has no ketubah implies needs get as Beit Chadash there see there; see Beit Meir from this.`,
  "4#_": `And found on her, etc. See responsum Noda BiYehudah Tinyana siman 56 regarding divorced woman who said she is widow and priest married her; after nissuin when priest knew she is divorced fled from her and left her sitting desolate as fit; priest died on way and has brother over seventy and location unknown; woman sits agunah many days; student wished to open gates of permission; he answered all his words have no substance; his view kiddushin certainly effective though he did not recognize her; nevertheless does not decide his reasoning certainly kiddushin even leniently not kiddushin another captures her; but even if betrothed doubtful does not help her that chalutz unknown location — we give him presumption of life and this woman will be agunah until clear matter from yavam see there; there siman 162 expanded more and concluded consensus of all poskim chayavei lavin and he did not recognize her — kiddushin take effect see there. See responsum Chut HaMeshulash in omissions to siman 221 who wrote: incident — one brought daughter, set dowry 200 gold for prominent rich man from distant community who came; immediately after kiddushin entering father's house which was wedding house — known exchange document forged and did not put even perutah besides 200. No person doubted kiddushin and I too God forbid doubt; only nevertheless in days of Geonim or early great ones appears room for great one of generation to dispute and say Gemara did not forbid (see above siman 38 seif 35) only in their days when kiddushin long before nissuin, etc. Even for Rambam who holds betrothing without specification and vow or blemish found needs get from doubt — implies certainly even if not married and no relations — one can say specifically vows and blemishes perhaps this man not particular — if particular would stipulate as people stipulate; unlike giving dowry mentioned in conditions and all particular and we testify he betrothed with this intent. Still did not have relations lest say because not in Bava Batra he forgave everything. Reasoning even if had relations we do not say in such case he forgave — strange matter not in "found" we say entered his mind. Appears if afterward another betrothed her needs get from both end quote; see responsum Shivat Tzion siman 88 whether in such case can divorce against her will — brought below siman 52 s.k. 2 see there.`,
  "5#_": `Doubtfully betrothed. See responsum Galia Mesechet siman 5 who sides saying only regarding prohibition of forbidden relations they were stringent in doubt but not regarding rabbinic prohibition; brought proof from Beit Yosef Yoreh De'ah siman 228 what he brought there responsum Ramban and Rashba. Also doubted there on Beit Shmuel siman 117 s.k. 24 what he wrote we force her since she acted improperly see there. End wrote: I already answered similarly to sages of community Minsk in incident widow took woman from elsewhere; husband particular if she had previously how many men divorced her; woman said only one man; afterward known before nissuin she had two men; with difficulty agreed only on condition not married to men more than them; woman brought one witness who testified falsely before court per woman's words; after nissuin known she had many men — initially particular only on this; witness who lied in testimony misled him; matter approximately type of particularities of vows and blemishes fit to be particular; sages of aforementioned community ruled as blemishes found to exempt him from monetary obligation; agreed Maharatz of community Vilna; only entangled in rabbinic prohibition; I answered them all as said above; additionally room to permit there was false witness — can compare to ailonit as Tosafot beginning Yevamot and Gittin 46b: ailonit not common and did not enter his mind to have relations for kiddushin; likewise false witness not common and never entered his mind to forgive aforementioned particularity end quote see there; see previous s.k.`,
  "6#_": `And doctor healed them she is betrothed. Per Beit Shmuel who wrote possible even after known helps, etc.; however one can say when known he thought no cure and like vows (above seif 2) when known permission does not help because he thinks no permission end quote. See Sefer Birchas Yosef in name of Rav Maharach Alfandari: if husband doctor and knew they have cure — obvious betrothed for his intent they not remain and she knows they have cure see there; per this possibly likewise vows if husband sage — requires examination.`,
});

